fresh-eyes: independent cold reviewer (subagent, no authorship of this branch), full scope of the two commits making the gate refuse a review note that names no mutation.

Verdict LAND on this tip, 85ba0bd.

Two rounds. Round one returned DO-NOT-LAND on a regression the change
introduced: capturing the note into a variable made a missing note
fail silently under set -e, so the most common first-contact case
printed nothing. Fixed with || true on the capture.

Round two verified by execution under /bin/sh, dash, and a real
pre-push hook against a bare remote. Five note cases: none refused
with the fresh-eyes message; read-only refused with the mutation
message; a note naming a mutation accepted and the push lands; the two
negative phrasings accepted, as the header now documents. Exemptions
unchanged: pushing refs/notes/reviews accepted, a branch deletion
accepted, an annotated tag gated at the commit it peels to, a notes
object pushed at a branch refused and now with a message. Multi-ref
bad-then-good and good-then-bad both refuse and land nothing.

Mutation, killed by the probe: sed the || true back out on a no-note
tip, and the grep count for the fresh-eyes message drops from 1 to 0.
Put back, 1. The token is load-bearing.

The stdin capture still precedes everything: a stub runner that eats
stdin leaves the loop with its ref. The set -e interaction with the
two greps that follow the now-always-succeeding assignment was checked
by reading: both sit inside if ! conditions, where set -e is inert.

The check is a claim check. It cannot verify a mutation was applied
any more than it can verify the reading. The header says so, beside
the other two known limits, and the reviewer called the framing honest
so long as a passing gate is never cited as evidence a mutation
happened.
